<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Russian Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Imperial Russia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Russian Empire was the state ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-romanov">Romanov dynasty</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a>'s proclamation of the empire in 1721 until the revolution of 1917. It expanded across eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> to become one of the largest empires in history. Its expansion into Central Asia also made it a strategic factor in the calculations of neighbouring powers, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/russian-empire.jpg" alt="Russian Empire" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter the Great · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Russia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It remained part of Qajar Iran until the Russo-Persian wars of 1804–1813 and 1826–1828, when the treaties of Gulistan and Turkmenchay ceded the khanates north of the Aras River to the <b>Russian Empire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Advised by Grigory Potemkin, Catherine II proclaimed the annexation of Crimea to the <b>Russian Empire</b> on 8 April 1783, and Şahin Giray abdicated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Law was codified in three Statutes of Lithuania (1529, 1566 and 1588), among the most advanced legal codes of their time; the third remained in force in parts of the <b>Russian Empire</b> until 1840.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>…the end of the grand duchy as a political form and the beginning of the Tsardom of Russia, which conquered Kazan in 1552 and Astrakhan in 1556, became the <b>Russian Empire</b> under Peter the Great in 1721 and passed on Moscow's autocratic traditions to the modern Russian state.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The alliance between Solomon I and Erekle II of Kartli-Kakheti in the eighteenth century, and their shared turn towards Russia, prepared the incorporation of Georgia into the <b>Russian Empire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-imereti">Kingdom of Imereti · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Qajars came to power with a tribal army of cavalry, but they faced the modern armies of the <b>Russian Empire</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran · Military</a></li></ul></section>
